The '''Upheaval and Reform period (1875–1905)''' covers the conclusion of the [[Libertad-Lacashe War]], the expansion of the Guajicutean bureaucracy through a licensing scheme for mining and government services, the rise of an urban multi-racial workers' party, the women's suffrage movement, environmental protectionist movements, the rediscovery of the pre-colonial catacombs, and the [[Second War of the Isthmus]], which brought [[Zong]] into Guajicutea's trade sphere. The period was marked by two concurrent tensions: racial contention and multiculturalism, as the constitution was amended to expand rights for all races (1897) and to enfranchise women (1904). Growing foreign ownership of Guajicutean industry and resources fueled nationalist sentiment that cut across party lines.

== Libertad-Lacashe War (1875–1880) ==

The [[Libertad-Lacashe War]], which began in 1875 when [[Lacashe]] invaded [[Libertalia]], concluded in 1880 with a peace treaty reimposing the partition and guaranteeing Libertalia's independence. [[Asikyira]] deployed a substantial army that pushed Lacashian forces back across the border. Guajicutea's direct involvement was limited, but the war occupied regional attention and shaped the political climate at the start of the period.

== Greater bureaucracy and licensing scheme ==

In the early 1880s, the Guajicutean government established a greater bureaucracy centred on a licensing scheme for gold mining, guano extraction, and the handling of government services. Since the [[Republic of Guajicutea|first republic]] (1824), gold mining had been run by the state, but the operation was poorly maintained: constant looting and corruption in the industry made state-run mining ineffective. By this point, most of the easily accessible gold in the northern highlands had been depleted through centuries of panning and mining, while gold in the south, along the rivers of [[Monte Yukumá]], was difficult to extract due to terrain and depth.

Unable to manage mining effectively, the government abandoned direct operation and shifted to a licensing system. Private operators paid license fees for the right to mine gold and guano. Some government services were also licensed out to private operators. The scheme created a new bureaucratic apparatus of officials, inspectors, and license offices.

In 1885, the '''National Census Bureau''' was established as part of the bureaucracy expansion, conducting the first systematic census of Guajicutea's population and employment. Subsequent censuses were conducted every five years, providing the first reliable data on the Guajicutean workforce and its sectoral distribution.

However, mining licenses were predominantly given to foreign entities rather than local operators. This pattern of foreign control over Guajicutean resources contributed to a growing sentiment that the nation was giving up too much to foreigners.

== Ironworks closure and foreign acquisition ==

The ironworks established in [[Waribá]] in 1864 by the [[Sarta|Sartan]] government and transferred to the Guajicutean government to operate had depended on iron ore imported from [[Tapuya]], as iron was not mined locally. The import dependence made the operation expensive and logistically vulnerable. By the 1880s, the ironworks was losing money on Tapuyan imports and proved unsustainable. The ironworks shut down and was subsequently purchased by a Volisanian steel company, marking a significant instance of foreign acquisition of domestic industrial assets.

The pattern of foreign ownership — Sartan investors had built the ironworks, it failed under Guajicutean government operation, and a Volisanian company acquired it — reinforced the sentiment that Guajicutea was losing control of its own industry and resources to foreign interests.

== Labour and workers' parties ==

The continued industrialisation of the 1870s and 1880s produced a growing urban working class. In 1883, the [[Partido de los Trabajadores]] (PdT) was founded as an urban, multi-racial workers' party. Unlike the [[Partido de Estabilización|PpE]], which was nativist and anti-immigration, the PdT organised across racial lines, seeking to unite urban workers of all backgrounds — Guaji, Bayani, Kaftian, and Sartan — around shared labour interests. The PdT was distinct from the [[Partido por la Tierra|PpLT]] as well: the PpLT's base was rural and agricultural, while the PdT focused on the growing industrial workforce in [[Waribá]] and [[Tabakurí]].

The emergence of the PdT pressured the PpLT to adopt stronger labour positions. To prevent the PdT from capturing urban workers, the PpLT pushed leftward, embracing more radical labour protections and welfare policies. This competitive dynamic between the PpLT and PdT shaped the reform agenda of the 1890s and 1900s.

== Women's rights and the Movimiento de la Warí ==

Around 1885, a women's rights movement emerged from the nunneries of Guajicutea. The nunneries were institutional descendants of the pre-colonial priestess tradition: the [[Warí]] had held ultimate authority in pre-colonial Guajicutean society, with the priestess outranking the king in disputes. The movement took the name [[Movimiento de la Warí]], invoking the ancient priestess title as precedent for women's political power. The framing was not that women's suffrage was a new grant, but a restoration of authority that had been taken from them.

The cultural revival of the 1890s, spurred by the rediscovery of the catacombs in 1892, made this historical framing resonate more broadly. Women's economic independence had also grown with industrialisation, as many women left agricultural labour for factory work in Waribá. The movement culminated in the [[Constitutional Amendment of 1904]], which enfranchised women. The amendment was a PpLT achievement, passed during the party's period of full legislative and executive control.

== Environmental protectionist movements ==

By the late 1880s, the environmental effects of industrialisation and mining had become visible. In the south, pollution from ironworks, manufacturing, and mining operations degraded land and water. In the north, forests were being slowly cleared for logging and mining operations. The earliest protectionist movements were led by native Guajis still living traditional lives, along with farmers whose livelihoods were directly affected by deforestation and pollution.

The environmental divide mapped onto party lines: the PpLT tended toward protection, while the PdI favoured industrial development. The licensing of mining operations to foreign entities, who had less incentive to mine carefully than the state, exacerbated the environmental damage.

== Racial politics and the Constitutional Amendment of 1897 ==

The period was defined by two concurrent tensions: racial contention and multiculturalism. Anti-Kaftian sentiment persisted from the previous era, but a counter-movement for equal rights gained strength, driven by the PpLT's leftward shift and the PdT's multi-racial coalition.

The [[Constitutional Amendment of 1897]] expanded rights and protections for all races and peoples in Guajicutea. While the [[Constitutional Amendment of 1803|1803 amendment]] had granted universal citizenship, the 1897 amendment went further, establishing constitutional prohibitions on discrimination in employment, housing, and public services, with enforcement mechanisms. The amendment was a PpLT initiative, pushed through the [[Asamblea Nacional]] during a period of PdI war leadership. The PpLT, then in opposition, used its legislative seats to drive the amendment through, and the PdI president signed it into law.

== Political development ==

=== Assembly expansion (1900) ===

In 1900, the [[Asamblea Nacional]] was expanded from 32 seats to 150 seats. The expansion, reflecting population growth to approximately 325,000 and democratic reform pressure, apportioned seats by population as established by the [[Constitutional Amendment of 1803|1803 amendment]]. The expansion was intended to make the assembly more representative, but with six or more parties competing for seats in the following years, it produced extreme fragmentation that would paralyse Guajicutean politics through the next decade.

=== PpLT corruption scandals ===

Around 1888, corruption scandals involving the PpLT weakened the party's position. PpLT officials had been taking kickbacks in exchange for granting or expediting mining and guano licenses under the licensing scheme established earlier in the decade. The irony was that the licensing scheme had been a PpLT initiative, and party officials were exploiting their own creation. The scandals ended the PdI-PpLT secret coalition that had governed since 1872 and allowed the PdI to gain a slight majority.

=== PdI war leadership ===

The PdI held leadership during the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899), though only with a slight majority in the Asamblea. During this period, the PdI established an income tax system to stabilise government coffers, which had previously relied on excise taxes, light tariffs on manufactured products, and licensing fees. The income tax provided a new revenue source at a time when war expenditures strained the budget. The licensing fee system had proven both insufficient and vulnerable to corruption.

=== Post-war recession and the Mild Depression ===

The war's end in 1899 produced an immediate economic recession. During the war, Guajicutean exports had benefited from increased demand and elevated prices. With the conclusion of hostilities, demand for exports dropped, prices fell, and the downturn was felt most acutely in [[Waribá]], where the industrial sector had expanded during the war years, and in the southern agricultural districts, where plantation output had been redirected to supply war-era trade.

The post-war recession merged into a broader economic contraction known as the '''Mild Depression''' (1901–1908). Guajicutea's interconnected economy — deeply integrated into regional trade networks through exports, imports of raw materials, and foreign-owned industry — made it disproportionately vulnerable to external shocks. When the broader regional economy contracted, demand for Guajicutean exports collapsed further. The income tax system established earlier in the decade helped maintain government revenue through the downturn, but unemployment rose, wages stagnated, and the depression persisted long after the war's end.

In 1905, the depression worsened sharply when the Volisanian-owned ironworks in [[Waribá]] shut down. The ironworks — originally established by the Sartan government in 1864, transferred to the Guajicutean government, and later purchased by a Volisanian steel company — had been the island's principal heavy industrial installation. Its closure threw hundreds of industrial workers out of employment in a city already suffering from the depression, triggering mass protests across Waribá.

The economic downturn caused the Asamblea to flip to the PpLT, while the PdI retained the presidency until 1901. In the 1901 election, the PpLT won the presidency as well, gaining full control of the government.

=== PpLT reforms ===

With full control of the legislature and executive from 1901, the PpLT pushed through its reform agenda. The party had already driven the 1897 amendment from opposition; now in power, it pursued the 1904 amendment for women's enfranchisement. The PpLT's leftward shift, motivated by competition with the PdT for urban workers, produced the most significant constitutional reforms since the 1803 amendment.

The PpLT's labour reform package combined worker protections with business concessions. The party instituted a 40-hour work week and abolished child labour, landmark protections for industrial and agricultural workers. However, the same package banned strikes: workers' unions were required to negotiate with employers but were prohibited from striking. The ban was a concession to business interests, ensuring that the new protections would not be enforced through work stoppages. Workers could bargain, but they could not withhold their labour.

The strike ban was tested in 1904 when industrial workers in [[Waribá]] struck in defiance of the law. The [[Huelga de Waribá de 1904]] was the first police-worker strike interaction in Guajicutean history. Police were dispatched to break up the strike, resulting in clashes with workers. The event marked a turning point: the PpLT, the party that had pushed left to represent workers, was now using state force against them. The strike and its suppression deepened the political fault lines that the Mild Depression had opened, driving workers toward the [[Partido Socialista de Guaji|Socialist Party]] and hardening the resolve of the [[Nuevo Partido de Aislamiento|NPdA]] to organise in the streets.

== Second War of the Isthmus ==

The [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899) was fought between the [[Zong]]-led coalition of [[Louyang]], [[Sinchew]], and Zong against [[Lacashe]] for control of the [[Balboa]] isthmus. Zong forces occupied major Balboa ports by 1899. The war brought Zong into Guajicutea's geopolitical sphere. Immediately following the war's conclusion in 1899, Guajicutea established trade deals with Zong, opening a new commercial relationship.

The [[Volisania|Volisanian]] and [[Sarta|Sartan]] governments reacted negatively to Guajicutea's alignment with Zong. Both powers were beginning to explore fascist ideologies, and the Guaji-Zong trade relationship heightened their concern about losing influence in the Capuyaquiran region.

== Cultural revival ==

Interest in pre-colonial Guaji culture grew throughout the 1890s. In 1892, an expedition led by the explorer [[Samuel Fuentes]] rediscovered the [[Catacumbas del Abismo]], the pre-colonial catacombs whose existence was known but whose location had been lost. The catacombs contained the calendar corridor — a long wall of carved stone recording each year from 516 CE onward, with circles, tally marks, and pictographic markers for wars, eruptions, hurricanes, and deaths — and the storm ledger, a pictographic record of storms and disasters maintained by [[bohíques|bohíque]] scribes. The rediscovery brought actual [[Bikumaku]] glyphs, the pre-colonial Guaji writing system, into public view for the first time in centuries. Previously known only through oral tradition and scattered artifacts, the script was now visible in situ, carved into stone.

=== Bikuismo ===

The rediscovery of the catacombs transformed the proto-Symbolism movement that had been developing since the 1840s into a distinct artistic movement. Earlier proto-Symbolist painters such as [[Sebastián Biku]] and [[Ana Flores]] had drawn on Bikumaku glyphs and pre-colonial imagery from surviving artifacts and oral tradition. After 1892, artists could study the actual glyph forms in the catacombs — the logographic symbols, the phonetic modifier strokes, the calendar's circles and tally marks, the storm ledger's wave marks and spirals. The movement took the name '''Bikuismo''', from ''bikú'', the carved year-mark that was the fundamental unit of the Bikumaku calendar system.

Bikuismo was characterised by the direct incorporation of Bikumaku script and native Guaji visual language into contemporary art. Glyphs appeared as compositional elements in paintings, reproduced in newspapers, and incorporated into decorative arts and architecture. The pictographic vocabulary of the catacombs — crossed spears for war, spirals for hurricanes, feathered figures for deaths, clasped hands for unification — provided a shared symbolic repertoire that artists drew on across painting, printmaking, and decorative arts. The visual language of pre-colonial material culture, including carved stone patterns, pottery motifs, and the three powers symbolism of sea, mountain, and sky, entered mainstream Guajicutean art. Traditional carving techniques were adapted for new contexts.

The movement differed from the earlier proto-Symbolism in its relationship to source material. Proto-Symbolism had been interpretive, drawing on memory and oral tradition to evoke pre-colonial themes. Bikuismo was documentary, working from actual glyphs and carvings that artists could study directly. The script was no longer an influence but a material — reproduced, rearranged, and displayed in works that asserted the continuity of Guaji civilisation.

The cultural revival connected to the political movements of the period. The [[Movimiento de la Warí]] drew on the rediscovered catacombs to ground its arguments for women's political authority in documented historical evidence: the calendar corridor and storm ledger were maintained by priestesses, and the Warí's authority was carved in stone. The revival also reinforced the broader reform agenda by demonstrating the depth and sophistication of pre-colonial Guaji civilisation, undercutting narratives that had marginalised native culture as primitive.

The expansion of newspapers from the early 1880s onward, driven by rising literacy from the 1870 public education act, accelerated the cultural revival. Newspapers printed reproductions of catacombs glyphs, reported on the expedition's findings, and spread information about pre-colonial culture to a readership that now included working-class Guaji. The intersection of mass media, rising literacy, and the rediscovery of the catacombs made Bikuismo a popular phenomenon rather than an elite one.

== Rise of new political movements ==

The Mild Depression's prolonged economic hardship produced two new political parties that would come to dominate Guajicutean politics in the following decades, both in elections and in the streets.

The '''[[Partido Socialista de Guaji]]''' (PSG) emerged from the labour unrest and economic dislocation of the depression. Distinct from the [[Partido de los Trabajadores|PdT]], which was a broad multi-racial workers' coalition, the Socialist Party pursued a more explicitly socialist programme. The depression's impact on industrial workers in [[Waribá]] and on agricultural labourers in the southern districts created a constituency that the existing parties struggled to represent. The PpLT's leftward shift had absorbed some of this pressure, but the depression pushed the political spectrum further left than the PpLT could reach while maintaining its rural base.

The '''[[Nuevo Partido de Aislamiento]]''' (NPdA) emerged from the economic nationalism that had been building throughout the period. Fascist ideologies transmitting from [[Volisania]] and [[Sarta]] through cultural channels from around 1901 found a receptive audience among those who felt the nation had given up too much to foreign interests — foreign mining licenses, the Volisanian acquisition of the ironworks, and the new Guaji-Zong trade relationship. The NPdA adopted the name of the old [[Partido de Aislamiento|PdA]] (the isolationist party of the 1830s–1840s) but with a fundamentally different ideology. Where the PdA had been a conventional isolationist party driven by post-trauma caution, the NPdA was a fascist party: ultranationalist, protectionist, anti-foreign, and organised for street-level mobilisation as well as electoral politics.

Both parties found their constituencies in the depression's dislocation. The PSG organised among industrial workers and the unemployed. The NPdA organised among those who blamed foreign influence and immigrant labour for the economic crisis. The two parties would come to dominate Guajicutean politics in the coming decades, their competition shaping the political landscape through both elections and street-level confrontation.

=== Noche de las Camisas Rojas ===

The mass protests triggered by the 1905 ironworks closure radicalised elements of the workers' movement. In the midst of the protests, communist-backed forces inspired by the [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]] (1904–1905) attempted to seize a police station in Waribá. The event, known as the '''[[Noche de las Camisas Rojas]]''' (Night of the Red Shirts), was the first armed confrontation between organised political radicals and state security forces in Guajicutean history. The attempt was repelled, but it marked a new escalation in political violence: from labour disputes and police clashes to organised political insurrection. The event hardened state response and deepened the polarisation between the emerging socialist and fascist movements, foreshadowing the street-level conflicts that would define the coming decades.

== Demographics ==

The population of Guajicutea grew from approximately 250,000 in 1875 to approximately 340,000 by 1905, a growth rate of approximately 1.1% per year, slightly slower than the previous era due to the post-war recession.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Group !! c. 1875 !! % !! c. 1905 !! % !! Notes
|-
| Guaji || ~138,000 || 55% || ~186,000 || 55% || Natural increase. Stable share.
|-
| Bayani || ~37,000 || 15% || ~48,000 || 14% || Natural increase.
|-
| Sartan / Sartan-Guaji mixed || ~31,000 || 12% || ~37,000 || 11% || Lower birth rates. Some emigration.
|-
| Kaftian || ~36,000 || 14% || ~50,000 || 15% || Natural increase + small trickle despite 1866 ban.
|-
| Other || ~8,000 || 3% || ~15,000 || 4% || Growing — Zong traders post-1899, Volisanian.
|-
| '''Total''' || '''~250,000''' || || '''~336,000''' || ||
|}

The Kaftian share increased slightly from 14% to 15%, driven by natural increase and a small continued trickle of arrivals despite the 1866 immigration ban. The "Other" category grew from 3% to 4%, reflecting the arrival of Zong traders after the Second War of the Isthmus.

=== Urbanisation ===

Urbanisation continued to accelerate, approaching 50% by the end of the period:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Year !! Urban !! % !! Rural !! % !! Notes
|-
| 1875 || ~107,000 || 43% || ~143,000 || 57% ||
|-
| 1905 || ~165,000 || 49% || ~175,000 || 51% ||
|}

[[Waribá]] remained the largest city, growing to approximately 70,000 residents, driven by the port, the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]], and manufacturing. [[Tabakurí]] grew to approximately 42,000, while [[Barikú]] reached approximately 28,000.

